<article class="env-lemma" id="0BAI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BAI">Lemma <span data-tag="0BAI">29.52.5</span>.</a> Let $X$, $Y$ be schemes. Let $f : X \to Y$ be locally of finite type. Let $X^0$, resp. $Y^0$ denote the set of generic points of irreducible components of $X$, resp. $Y$. Assume </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$X^0$ and $Y^0$ are finite and $f^{-1}(Y^0) = X^0$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
either $f$ is quasi-compact or $f$ is separated. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then there exists a dense open $V \subset Y$ such that $f^{-1}(V) \to V$ is finite. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Since $Y$ has finitely many irreducible components, we can find a dense open which is a disjoint union of its irreducible components. Thus we may assume $Y$ is irreducible affine with generic point $\eta $. Then the fibre over $\eta $ is finite as $X^0$ is finite. 
    <p>
      Assume $f$ is separated and $Y$ irreducible affine. Choose $V \subset Y$ and $U \subset X$ as in Lemma <a href="/tag/02NW" data-tag="02NW">29.52.1</a> part (3). Since $f|_ U : U \to V$ is finite, we see that $U \subset f^{-1}(V)$ is closed as well as open (Lemmas <a href="/tag/01W6" data-tag="01W6">29.42.7</a> and <a href="/tag/01WN" data-tag="01WN">29.45.11</a>). Thus $f^{-1}(V) = U \amalg W$ for some open subscheme $W$ of $X$. However, since $U$ contains all the generic points of $X$ we conclude that $W = \emptyset $ as desired. 
    <p>
      Assume $f$ is quasi-compact and $Y$ irreducible affine. Then $X$ is quasi-compact, hence there exists a dense open subscheme $U \subset X$ which is separated (Properties, Lemma <a href="/tag/03J1" data-tag="03J1">28.30.3</a>). Since the set of generic points $X^0$ is finite, we see that $X^0 \subset U$. Thus $\eta \not\in f(X \setminus U)$. Since $X \setminus U \to Y$ is quasi-compact, we conclude that there is a nonempty open $V \subset Y$ such that $f^{-1}(V) \subset U$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/01RL" data-tag="01RL">29.8.3</a>. After replacing $X$ by $f^{-1}(V)$ and $Y$ by $V$ we reduce to the separated case which we dealt with in the preceding paragraph. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>